3. The discrete element approach
3.1. Introduction
In order to improve the thermal predictions with the equivalent sand grain approach, a large data set of the relative evolutions of the temperature and velocity shifts is thus required. Experimental data sets, including velocity and temperature profiles, are quite rare so that numerical simulations have to be used to cover a wide enough range of parameters. As pointed out in the introduction, only ‘‘high fidelity’’ solutions capturing the exact roughness geometry or the discrete element approach are able to reproduce the difference between drag and heat transfer enhancement by surface roughness. As ‘‘high fidelity’’ computations are quite expensive while rather reliable results can be obtained at a very low cost with the discrete elements approach, the discrete element approach was favoured.
3.2. The ‘‘classical’’ discrete element approach
Schlichting (1936, 1973) pointed out that the rôle of roughness elements is a flow blockage together with drag and heat flux on the roughness elements. To mimic these phenomena, Robertson (1961) developed the discrete element approach, which was popularized by Finson (see e.g. Finson (1982) and references herein). After some debates on the correct form of the equations, Coleman et al. (1983) and Taylor et al. (1985) proposed, from a balance over a volume including roughness elements, a set of equations which is now considered as the standard form of the discrete element approach. For a steady, two-dimensional boundary layer, respectively noting x and y the flow and ‘‘wall normal’’ directions and u and v the velocity components along these directions, the equations read
