3.3. Improvements of the ‘‘classical’’ approach
The initial model form, as given above, assumes that all roughness elements have the same shape and are uniformly distributed. The approach was extended by Taylor et al. (1988) to cope with a regular mixture of different shapes of roughness elements and by McClain et al. (2004), using probability density functions, to more random roughness elements. In the initial model form, only roughness elements of circular cross-section can be accounted for. McClain et al. (2004) proposed to deal with elements of elliptical cross-section, which can be a fair approximation in many practical cases. Defining the eccentricity e as the ratio, at every altitude y, between the width of the element in the direction normal to the flow and its length in the flow direction, the drag coefficient is multiplied by e0:73456, based upon cylinder drag data. Due to the lack of data, the heat transfer correction is simply the perimeter ratio between an ellipse and a circle. Although used in the following, this correction has to be taken with caution as it lacks direct experimental validation. At last, Klett and Kithcart (1992) and McClain et al. (2006) pointed out the failure of the approach for dense roughness, drag and heat transfer being overestimated. McClain et al. noticed that the lower part of the roughness elements should be submitted to a very weak flow and have little influence. Defining the reference surface (or melt-down surface) as the smooth surface corresponding to the same amount of material, i.e. the mean elevation which could be obtained by locally melting the roughness elements, they propose to replace the real surface by the corrected surface i.e. the real surface where it is above the reference surface, the reference surface otherwise, in order to get rid of the contribution of the troughs between roughness elements. Although not discussed by McClain et al., this change of bottom surface also leads to a change in the altitude and thus in the mixing length, which reduces the turbulence levels and thus the drag and heat transfer.
3.4. Going further
The discrete element approach was also applied with other turbulence models than the mixing length model by Tarada (1990) and by Glikson and Aupoix (1996). Using the volume averaging technique proposed by Whitaker (1986) together with the triple decomposition of Raupach
