4.3. Simulation results
In order to build a reference database of roughness effects, various roughness element shapes were considered, namely hemispheres, pairs of hemispheres of different diameters, cones with various half angle (30°, 45° and 60°), cones of 30° half angle with spherical covers of various extend (labelled ‘‘SC’’ in the figure captions), ellipsoids with circular or elliptic cross-section in planes parallel to the reference surface and various eccentricities (respectively labelled ‘‘Ellips.’’ and ‘‘Excent’’ in the figure captions). For each roughness element shape, different spacing were selected, from the densest one to very sparse roughness configurations.
This led to a total of 99 different geometries. For each geometry, 50 test cases were computed, for reduced roughness height hþ from 10 to 500 with a step of 10, which led to 4950 simulations. Fig. 2 gives a typical example of the relative evolution of the velocity and temperature shifts in the case of hemispheres (all cases are not plotted for the sake of clarity). A curve corresponds to a given spacing L=h. As all curves stop for a reduced height hþ 1⁄4 500, it can be seen that the most closely packed hemispheres (L=h 1:86) do not give the largest velocity shift Duþ, i.e. the rougher surface. The maximum velocity shift Duþ first increases as the hemispheres are less dense, the maximum being achieved for L=h 2:50. Above this value, dynamical roughness effects decrease. The key result is that, in all cases, there is not a simple linear relation between the velocity and temperature shift. There seems to be an asymptote for small roughness effects (Duþ ! 0) and then a parabolic behaviour, the temperature shift finally decreasing while the velocity shift keeps on increasing. For low values of the velocity shift, the proportionality between the velocity and temperature shifts is consistent with Kays and Crawford formula (12) and results from Pimenta et al. (1975), Ligrani and Moffat (1986) or Hosni et al. (1989), which all considered cases for which the velocity shift Duþ is below eight. The decrease of the temperature shift reflects the different nature of drag and heat transfer increases due to roughness and is consistent with the negative temperature shifts discussed above. When the shielding effect of roughness elements is weaker, e.g. for cones, the maximum roughness effect is obtained for the denser configuration. Whatever the roughness element shape, the parabolic behaviour of the temperature shift versus the velocity shift is recovered. As the heat transfer increase is linked to the increase of the wetted surface, the ratio of the rough wetted surface to the smo