5. Thermal correction development
As pointed out above, the roughness modelling in the framework of the equivalent sand grain approach is unphysical: the pressure force on the roughness elements is replaced by an increase in the turbulence level to achieve the same wall friction. Therefore, the velocity profiles in the roughness layer significantly differ between the equivalent sand grain and the discrete element approaches. Consequently, there is no simple way to directly derive a thermal correction from the knowledge of e.g. temperature profiles in the roughness layer, only an ad-hoc correction to recover the correct heat flux level, or more precisely the correct temperature shift, can be considered.
As the eddy viscosity is already altered in the equivalent sand grain approach, the only available tool to improve the heat flux prediction is a modification of the turbulent Prandtl number. Increasing the turbulent Prandtl number decreases the turbulent conductivity and hence the heat transfer towards the wall and the heat flux. Therefore, a correction is looked for with the form
