6. Thermal correction validation
Model validation was performed using the ONERA boundary layer code CLICET (Aupoix, 2010) which has a self-adaptive grid and several grid levels so that grid convergence is easily ensured. Academic roughness as well as rough surfaces representative of iced airfoil or in-service turbine blades and vanes were considered, together with pressure gradient effects. Forty-six test-cases were computed; only the most significant ones are presented here for the sake of brevity. Test cases in the fully rough regime (kþ
s > 100), with significant roughness effects, are favoured. Computations were performed with all roughness corrections developed using ONERA’s strategy but, for the sake of clarity, results are shown only for the Spalart and Allmaras (Aupoix and Spalart, 2003) (curves labelled ‘‘SA’’ in the figures) and k x SST models, the ‘‘Colebrook’’ type correction (Aupoix, 2015) being used for this model (curves labelled SST). Classical computations, using a constant turbulent Prandtl number, are labelled as ‘‘Constant Pt’’ while those using the present correction are labelled ‘‘Variable Pt’’.
