6.3. Ice shapes
Dukhan et al. (1999) copied ice shape obtained in NASA’s Icing Research Tunnel. Seven surfaces, corresponding to different icing regimes, were investigated. The experimental set up is a flat plate, with a smooth part 0.0508 m long before the rough insert. The smooth part is adiabatic while the rough element is heated. Several free-stream velocities were investigated but only the highest velocity case of 47.24 ms 1 is considered, the low velocity case being polluted by transition effects. The laminar/turbulent transition is assumed to take place at the beginning of the rough insert. Only Stanton number data are available, they are determined from a thermal balance in the steady regime. The mean height and spacing is given for each surface. The equivalent sand grain is deduced using Dirling (1973) correlation. This correlation requires another parameter, linked to the slope of the roughness element on the windward face. This parameter highly affects the results for dense roughness, so only sparse roughness cases are presented here. The wetted area ratio is also provided. As the corrected wetted area ratio is always lower, as shown in Fig. 3, it is limited to 1.22, maximum value for hemispheres. This value is the one used for the first two cases presented below. For the intermediately spaced rough glaze (shape 3 in Fig. 10), the equivalent sand grain height is 15.63 mm and the reduced value kþ
