7. Conclusions and perspectives
The discrete element approach was used easily to generate a large database to investigate the differences between skin friction and heat transfer enhancement on a rough surface. For that purpose, a large variety of roughness element shape, spacings and heights was investigated. Different behaviours of the velocity and temperature profile shifts in the logarithmic region were evidenced. This simply generated database provided a much larger information than available in the literature but no simple laws could be deduced from it. It however puts in evidence the need of an extra parameter to quantify thermal effects and the importance of the corrected wetted surface. A correction of the Prandtl number was deduced, only using this database. A correction of the turbulent Prandtl number in the wall region was looked for to reproduce the fact that the heat transfer increases less than the drag. The correction is made of two parts, a term which restricts it to the roughness layer and a maximum level which depends upon the roughness dynamic effect through the velocity shift Duþ and upon the corrected wetted surface ratio. The final model form is given by Eq. (29). Although this correction does not perfectly collapse all the database data, it brings significant improvements in the predictions over a wide variety of tests cases, although the heat flux remains slightly overestimated in some cases. It has to be recalled that this correction was developed without any calibration w.r.t. experimental data used in the validation. Moreover, this correction can be used with every roughness correction developed using ONERA’s strategy. Its weak point is the need to evaluate the corrected wetted surface ratio. Two ways of improvement can be envisioned. Now that a correction form is stated, the coefficients could be optimised w.r.t. the available experimental database used in the validation. Another route could be to get rid of the discrete element approach and use ‘‘higher fidelity’’ solutions, with more general surface roughness, to generate a more reliable (but much more expensive) database.
